A||||6||Fiero_24||Coolant temp running high||||5||
Z||000000||Fiero_24||06-12-2001||12:25 PM||||Sorry this is a long story, but I want to give all the info I can.  Last week I was driving to work and I saw smoke comming from the engine.  I saw the temp gauge going up, so I pulled over.  One of my rubber coolant pipes had dry rotted and split.  No big deal, so I took it in and had all the rubber coolant pipes replaced.  Ever since then the car has run a little hotter than before the hose splitting, and the temp going up faster than normal on short drives (no overheating though, I just hit the max A/C button).  When I put my sunroof glass under the hood yesterday, I noticed the coolant overflow tank was missing the cap.  I replaced the cap and noticed the temp stayed lower, but it was raining this morning and could not tell 100% if the cap was the fix.  Could the higher temps be a cause of the cap being left off?  Thanks.<P>Craig<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||sqoach||06-12-2001||12:30 PM||||I really don't believe that cap has anything to do with temperature. When the hose was replaced, was the system properly drained and refilled? There is a correct way to do this, and if done wrong could cause problems, like you're having. Go to Ogre's Cave. There's a section that shows proper re-filling of the water system.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fiero_24||06-12-2001||02:35 PM||||They explained how they drained the system on a fiero and fill it, and they were the only place out of 5 mechanics that explained it correctly by the angle at which you need to put the car at.  There were 2 other Fiero's there too.  It was not flushed, but was supposedly drained.  I did not think that the cap had anything to do with it, but I do know that the coolant recovery tank is under pressure.  I will check out the Ogres Cave, thanks for the info.  <P>Craig<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero_24 (edited 06-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||06-13-2001||08:34 AM||||At no time is the recovery tank under pressure! If you look at the cap for the tank you will notice that it does indeed vent to atmosphere with no resistance (if it didn't it would explode!). I agree with sqoach, sounds like an air bubble. Even when system is filled correctly an air pocket can exist from an empty heater core. At what temps is your car running and is you fan cutting in at the correct temp?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fiero_24||06-13-2001||01:44 PM||||Thanks.  I am going to try and get the air out of the system this weekend.  I know very little about the cooling system.<P>Craig||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||06-14-2001||07:22 AM||||Just as a point of interest, AutoZone, PepBoys, etc. all list the Fiero radiator cap as being VENTED.  This is incorrect.  It uses a NON-VENTED cap.<P>A vented cap will let coolant siphon from the radiator through the overflow bottle onto the ground when it is parked with the nose slightly lower than the rest of the car.||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||06-14-2001||10:42 PM||||I installed a lever vent cap two years ago. makes burping the system a breeze.||||reg||1
